# Git commits

Commit on your own after completing individual tasks, fixes, or review items — do not wait for the user to ask.

## When to commit

- One commit per logical task or fix (not one giant commit at the end)
- After finishing a review fix, commit that fix before moving to the next
- Include formatting-only changes (e.g. `cargo fmt`) in the same commit as the related code change

## Format

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/): `type(scope): subject`

Common types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `docs`, `chore`, `ci`, `perf`

## The juxtaposition

Every commit is a **stupid subject, serious body** bit:

- **Subject**: unhinged gen-z slang — brainrot, meme-y, lowercase chaos. The dumber the better.
- **Body**: crisp, professional engineering prose. What changed, why, trade-offs. Complete sentences. Could go in a PR description or postmortem.

The contrast is the point. Subject sounds like a group chat; body sounds like a staff engineer wrote it.

```bash
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
fix(chat): bestie the websocket literally ate shit and died 💀

The handler assumed every inbound message included a user ID. Anonymous
viewer messages omitted the field and caused a panic in the parser.
Guard the optional field and skip processing when absent.

EOF
)"
```

More subject examples (body always stays normal):

- `feat(overlay): we cooked so hard the badge renderer is giving sigma grindset`
- `fix(auth): token refresh was lowkey feral and not in a good way`
- `refactor(db): yeeted duplicate query into one function no thoughts head empty`
- `chore(deps): bumped tokio bc living in 2023 is not the vibe`

## Safety

Follow the git safety protocol: never push to `main`, no `--no-verify`, no committing secrets, no amend unless explicitly allowed.
